WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:07.009
If you’ve ever sent an email, you’ve probably
noticed the Cc field at the top of your message.

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:08.720
But what is it?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.510
And what exactly is a Bcc?

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.090
Let’s find out.

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:17.769
‘CC’ means ‘Carbon Copy’.

00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:23.839
Before email was popular and mechanical typewriters
weren’t just for hipsters, the quickest

00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:30.689
way to make copies of a document was to use
a special sheet of paper called carbon paper.

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:37.400
You would load the carbon paper into the typewriter
along with two normal sheets of paper to produce

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.430
two identical copies of the original.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:45.000
This is where the phrase ‘carbon copy’
comes from.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:51.200
Using the Cc function in email sends an identical
copy of the email

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.710
to the person in the Cc field.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.580
Think of it like a digital carbon copy.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:04.170
Some people also call it a ‘courtesy copy’,
as, in the workplace, it is used to keep people

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:10.600
aware of information that doesn’t require
direct action or a response from them.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:16.500
If a message is directly to someone - or more
than one person - then you should put their

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.490
address in the main address field.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:24.350
For example, if you were emailing someone
in your team about a project your manager

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:30.201
was overseeing, you would put your team member
in the primary ‘To’ address field, and

00:01:30.201 --> 00:01:33.830
your manager in the ‘Cc’ address field.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:39.899
Everyone in the email thread will be able
to see who else is in the conversation and

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:45.009
whether they have been Cc’d or addressed directly.

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:53.499
However, you will not be able to see any people
who have been added to the email with the

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:56.249
Bcc function.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:02:00.049
Bcc means ‘Blind Carbon Copy’.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:07.609
It is used to inform people in the same way
as a normal Cc’d email, but without any

00:02:07.609 --> 00:02:09.849
of the other participants knowing.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:15.519
It might be useful if your director wants
to discreetly oversee a conversation, but

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:21.280
it’s used more commonly for sending messages
to large email lists.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:29.849
For example, a company might use the Bcc function
to email all its customers at once, so it

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:34.799
doesn't share all those email addresses with
the other participants.

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:40.360
